 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Performance:** Outperforms existing methods in novel view rendering quality and pose estimation accuracy on the Temples and ScanNet datasets [S1]. On the LLFF dataset, PSNR indicators for 3, 6, and 9 input views are 1-2 points higher, and nearly 1 point higher than ViP-NeRF for 3 input views [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Handles challenging camera trajectories; does not require COLMAP or precomputed poses [S2].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the monocular depth estimation network; performance may degrade if the depth prior is inaccurate.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the depth estimation network and the NeRF rendering time. Typically, each iteration involves a forward pass through the depth network and the NeRF.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the depth maps and point clouds for all frames, which can be memory-intensive for large datasets.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Outperforms existing methods in novel view rendering quality and pose estimation accuracy on the Temples and ScanNet datasets [S1]. On the LLFF dataset, it achieves 1-2 points higher PSNR for 3, 6, and 9 input views compared to baseline methods [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles challenging camera trajectories; does not require precomputed poses; leverages monocular depth priors for geometric constraints.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the monocular depth estimation network; may struggle with scale ambiguity; sensitive to large camera movements.
+*   **When to Use:** When camera poses are unknown or unreliable, and monocular depth estimation is feasible.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high-precision precomputed poses are available, or when the scene has significant textureless regions that confuse depth estimation.
 
 ## Background
-NoPe-NeRF was proposed by Bian et al. in 2023 [S2]. It was introduced to address the problem of training NeRF without precomputing camera poses, specifically for challenging camera trajectories [S1, S2]. The method builds on the idea of using monocular depth priors to constrain pose optimization, contrasting with methods that only use photometric loss or reverse view pose parameters through gradient descent [S2].
+NoPe-NeRF was proposed by Bian et al. in 2023 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of previous methods like SC-NeRF, which struggled with large camera movements [S2]. The method builds on the idea of using monocular depth priors to guide NeRF optimization, correcting shift and scale parameters during training [S1]. It is used in applications requiring novel view synthesis from uncalibrated image sequences, such as indoor scene reconstruction [S1, S2].
 
